<p class="eyebrow">Free local document conversion</p>
<h1>Word to Markdown converter</h1>
<p>Open a .docx, inspect the converted article, and take a Markdown file into your next editor.</p>
</header>
<!--WORKFLOW_NAVIGATION-->
<div class="desk-intro-details">
<p class="small">Free, with no account or upload. Supports common headings, emphasis, links, lists, and simple tables in plain .docx files up to 8 MiB. Conversion runs in this tab.</p>
<p id="sample" class="small">No document handy? <a href="/samples/word-to-markdown.docx" download="drafttopage-fictional-sample.docx">Download a fictional Word sample</a> and choose it above. Or <button id="start-sample" type="button" class="secondary">Try a fictional sample</button> to try the editor without a file import.</p>
<nav class="jump-links" aria-label="Word to Markdown guide"><a href="#word-markdown-guide">How it works</a><a href="#word-markdown-limits">Conversion limits</a><a href="#word-markdown-example">Example output</a><a href="#word-markdown-questions">Common questions</a></nav>
<details id="desk-more" class="desk-more"><summary>Paste writing, keep source notes, or use more tools</summary><div class="desk-start"><button id="start-draft" type="button" class="secondary">Paste text or Markdown</button><button id="start-formatted-import" type="button" class="secondary">Paste formatted writing</button><button id="start-import" type="button" class="secondary">Open article import</button><button id="start-notes" type="button" class="secondary">Start with notes</button><button id="toggle-focus" type="button" class="secondary" aria-pressed="false">Focus on article</button><button id="start-checks" type="button" class="secondary">Check draft</button></div></details>
<p class="small">Save a private backup before refreshing or leaving; there is no autosave. A Markdown export contains the article, not your private workspace notes. Nothing is published automatically.</p>
